Let's consider the parity transformation \(P\): \((\boldsymbol{x}\to\boldsymbol{-x},\boldsymbol{y}\to\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{z} \to\boldsymbol{z})\) on \(\boldsymbol{S}\) and \(\boldsymbol{R_{1}}\). Remember that spin \(\boldsymbol{S}\) transforms like angular momentum \(\boldsymbol{l}=\boldsymbol{p}\times\boldsymbol{r}\), we obtains the following after a little algebra,

\[P\boldsymbol{R_{1}}=\boldsymbol{R_{4}}\,, \tag{1.7a}\] \[P\boldsymbol{S}\ =\!\!\!-\boldsymbol{S}\,. \tag{1.7b}\]

Therefore, if we assume parity invariant (of cross section \(\,\mathrm{d}\sigma\)), (and using rotational invariance)

\[\mathrm{d}\sigma(\boldsymbol{S},\boldsymbol{R_{1}})=\,\mathrm{d} \sigma(\boldsymbol{-S},\boldsymbol{R_{4}})=\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\boldsymbol{S}, \boldsymbol{R_{2}})=\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\boldsymbol{-S},\boldsymbol{R_{3}})\,, \tag{1.8a}\] \[\mathrm{d}\sigma(\boldsymbol{S},\boldsymbol{R_{4}})=\,\mathrm{d} \sigma(\boldsymbol{-S},\boldsymbol{R_{1}})=\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\boldsymbol{S}, \boldsymbol{R_{3}})=\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\boldsymbol{-S},\boldsymbol{R_{2}})\,. \tag{1.8b}\]

Figure 1.4: Single spin asymmetry measurement (generic \(\phi\)).

From Equations (1.6) and (1.8),

\[\epsilon(-\phi) = \frac{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{4}})-\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(- \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{4}})}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{4}})+\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(- \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{4}})} \tag{1.9}\] \[= \frac{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{3}})-\,\mathrm{d}\sigma( \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{1}})}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{3}})+\,\mathrm{d}\sigma( \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{1}})}=-\epsilon(\phi)\,.\]

Together with the continuity of \(\epsilon(\phi)\), we arrive at the conclusion

\[\epsilon(\phi=0)=\epsilon(\phi=\pi)=0\,. \tag{1.10}\]

Similarly,

\[\epsilon(\pi-\phi) = \frac{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{2}})-\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(- \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{2}})}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{2}})+\,\mathrm{d}\sigma( -\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{2}})} \tag{1.11}\] \[= \frac{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{1}})-\,\mathrm{d}\sigma( \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{3}})}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{1}})+\,\mathrm{d}\sigma( \mathbf{S},\mathbf{R_{3}})}=\epsilon(\phi)\,.\]

Therefore \(\phi=\frac{\pi}{2}\) and \(\phi=-\frac{\pi}{2}\) are the location of extrema of \(\epsilon(\phi)\). If (beyond just the simple symmetry discussion here) we know that \(|\epsilon(\phi)|\) has only 2 zero-loci at \(\phi=0\) and \(\phi=\pi\), then we can conclude the \(|\epsilon(\phi)|\) reaches maximum at \(\phi=\frac{\pi}{2}\) and \(\phi=-\frac{\pi}{2}\).

#### Differential Cross Section

Any measurement of asymmetries in a collision experiment is incompletely without also measuring the cross section of the specific reaction in question. Only when an understanding of the cross section is well established can we proceed to discuss the meaning of any observed (or unobserved) asymmetries. This is more so for the inclusive \(\pi^{0}\) production measurement in the paper (\(\sqrt{(}s)=200\) GeV, at large psuedorapidity (\(\eta>3\)) region), when the corresponding \(p_{T}\) is of the order of a few GeV.

The differential cross section for two-initial-state collision is given by Equation (3.4.15) of [8]. Keep in mind that the invariant phase space volume is \(\frac{1}{E}\,\mathrm{d}^{3}\boldsymbol{p}\) for states on mass-shell (\(p^{2}+M^{2}=0\)). For inclusive measurement (only \(\pi^{0}\) is measured), all of the final states except \(\pi^{0}\) are integrated. The Lorentz-invariant differential cross-section is thus (Equation 37.38 of [9]) \(E\frac{\mathrm{d}^{3}\sigma}{\mathrm{d}^{3}\boldsymbol{p}}\). As experimentalists, we count the number of observed \(\pi^{0}\), \(N\). \(N\) should be proportional to the integrated luminosity for the period of data taking, \(L\). The invariant differential cross-section is

\[E\frac{\mathrm{d}^{3}\sigma}{\mathrm{d}^{3}\boldsymbol{p}}=\frac{\mathrm{d}^{ 3}\sigma}{d\phi dyp_{T}dp_{T}}=\frac{C_{recon}C_{conv}}{f_{\pi^{0}}}\frac{1}{ \hat{\mathcal{L}}}\frac{N_{\pi^{0}}}{p_{T}^{*}\Delta p_{T}\Delta\phi\Delta y }\,, \tag{1.12}\]

where \(y\) is the rapidity (Equation 1.13); \(\Delta y\) is the rapidity range; \(\Delta\phi\) is the azimuthal angle covered by the FPD detector; \(p_{T}\) is the transverse momentum of \(\pi^{0}\), and \(p_{T}^{*}\) is defined as the \(p_{T}\) for which the cross section equals its average over the bin; \(C_{reco}\) is a correction for the acceptance, reconstruction efficiency; \(C_{conv}\) is a correction for the conversion of decay photons (\(\pi^{0}\) to \(\gamma\gamma\) branching ratio is \((98.798\pm 0.032)\%\)); \(f_{\pi^{0}}\) is the trigger bias of the BBC coincidence trigger (the fraction of the inclusive \(\pi^{0}\) yield that satisfies the BBC coincidence trigger); and \(\hat{\mathcal{L}}\) is the integrated luminosity for the analyzed data set.

Remember that the definition of rapidity,

\[y=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{E+p_{z}}{E-p_{z}}\right)=\tanh^{-1}\left(\frac{p_ {z}}{E}\right)\,. \tag{1.13}\]

For \(p\gg m\) and \(\theta\gg 1/\gamma\), \(y\approx\eta\), where \(eta\) is the pseudorapidity

\[\eta=-\ln\left(\tan\frac{\theta}{2}\right)\,. \tag{1.14}\]For (p)FPD, for all practical purpose, \(y\approx\eta\).

We should emphasize that it is in its own right very interesting to compare the cross-section measurement of inclusive forward \(\pi^{0}\) production at RHIC with next-to-leading order (NLO) pQCD calculation [10].

### Motivation

The underlying theory that governs strong interaction is Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD), one of components of the Standard Model. The elementary particles in QCD are quarks and gluons. Among the striking features of QCD are asymptotic freedom (the coupling of QCD becomes smaller at higher energy scale) and confinement (quarks and gluons are confined inside hadrons). At very high energy, the smallness of QCD coupling means perturbative expansion is a good approximation. At lower energy, the coupling constant of QCD becomes large, thus non-perturbative effects become increasing important and perturbative expansion is not expected to provide a reliable solution. In addition, as a consequence of confinement, what we measure in experiments are hadrons, not quarks or gluons. Quarks and gluons in the final states of the elementary interactions will have to somehow fragment into hadrons, a process inherently non-perturbative. Therefore, according to QCD factorization theorem, what we measure in high energy hadron scattering experiments will be the convolution of parton distribution functions (which describe the constituents (quarks, gluons) of the initial state particles), hard scattering cross sections for elementary pQCD processes, and fragmentation functions (which tell how quarks and gluons fragment into hadrons).

We consider the following process of inclusive hadron production in hadron scattering:

\[A^{\uparrow}(P_{A})+B(P_{B})\to h(P_{h})+X, \tag{1.15}\]

where \(A^{\uparrow}\) is the transversely polarized proton, \(B\) is the unpolarized proton, \(X\) is the collection of unobservable particles from the reaction, and particle \(h\) is a \(\pi^{0}\) meson.

The hadron-hadron scattering process is schematically shown by (Figure 1.5):

Figure 1.5: Hadron-hadron scattering with inclusive production of a particle \(h\).

The Mandelstam variables are as usual:

\[s=(P_{A}+P_{B})^{2}\,,\qquad t=(P_{A}-P_{h})^{2}\,,\qquad u=(P_{B}-P_{h})^{2}\,, \tag{1.16}\]

and Feynman variable \(x_{F}\) is

\[x_{F}\equiv\frac{2P_{hL}}{\sqrt{s}}=\frac{t-u}{s}\,. \tag{1.17}\]

According to QCD factorization theorem, the differential cross-section for the reaction (1.15) can be formally written as

\[d\sigma=\sum_{abc}\sum_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime}\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}\rho^{a}_{ \alpha\alpha^{\prime}}f_{a}(x_{a})\otimes f_{b}(x_{b})\otimes d\hat{\sigma}_{ \alpha\alpha^{\prime}\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}\otimes{\cal D}^{\gamma\gamma^{ \prime}}_{h/c}(z)\,. \tag{1.18}\]

Here \(f_{a}\) (\(f_{b}\)) is the distribution function of parton \(a\) (\(b\)) inside the hadron \(A\) (\(B\)), \(\rho^{a}_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime}}\) is the spin density matrix of parton \(a\), \({\cal D}^{\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}_{h/c}(z)\) is the fragmentation matrix of parton \(c\) into hadron \(h\), and \(d\hat{\sigma}/d\hat{t}\) is the partonic cross-section:

\[\left(\frac{d\hat{\sigma}}{d\hat{t}}\right)_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime}\gamma\gamma ^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{16\pi\hat{s}^{2}}\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\beta\delta}{\cal M}_{ \alpha\beta\gamma\delta}{\cal M}^{*}_{\alpha^{\prime}\beta\gamma^{\prime} \delta}, \tag{1.19}\]

where \({\it M}_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}\) is the scattering amplitude of the elementary partonic process

\[a(k_{a})+b(k_{b})\to c(k_{c})+d(k_{d}). \tag{1.20}\]

and \(\hat{s}\) and \(\hat{t}\) are partonic Mandelstam invariants. (see Figure 1.6).

If the produced hadron \(h\) is unpolarized, or spinless, we would expect in the collinear case that matrix \({\cal D}^{\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}_{h/c}\) is diagonal, _i.e._, \({\cal D}^{\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}_{h/c}=\delta_{\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}\,{\cal D} _{h/c}\), where \({\cal D}_{h/c}\) is the unpolarized fragmentation function (see Subsection 1.4.3). Combined with helicity conservation in the partonic subprocess (\(\alpha+\beta=\gamma+\delta\) and \(\alpha^{\prime}+\beta=\gamma^{\prime}+\delta\)), we would then arrive at \(\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}\). Therefore, in (1.19) there is no dependence on the spin of hadron \(A\) in the leading twist (leading order pQCD and collinear case) of pQCD and all single-spin asymmetries are zero at leading twist. Higher twist terms would be suppressed (such as by the mass of quark, \(\frac{m_{q}}{p_{T}}\alpha_{s}\ll 1\)), and single-spin asymmetry for inclusive \(\pi\) production in polarized \(p\)-\(p\) collision was thus expected to be small. However, we know from experiments that there is large non-zero single-spin asymmetry \(A_{N}\) at large \(x_{F}\).

To summarize, it is necessary to look beyond the collinear terms at leading twist.

Figure 1.6: Elementary parton-parton scattering processes.

### Single Spin Asymmetries in \(p_{\uparrow}+p\to\pi^{0}+X\)

Remember that Equation (1.18) is for the collinear case and leading twist. To get non-zero single-spin asymmetries, we may either take into account the transverse motion of quarks inside hadrons (either the initial states or the final states), or consider higher twist terms. The following 3 models have been proposed:

1. Collins effect [11]: Intrinsic transverse momentum \(\mathbf{\kappa}_{T}\) of hadron \(h\) (\(\pi^{0}\)) relative to the fragmenting quark opens up the possibility that \({\cal D}_{h/c}^{\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}\) is not necessarily diagonal (owing to \(T\)-odd fragmentation functions). This effect also requires a non-zero value for the transversity structure function (also called transversity distribution), which represents the difference in probability to find a quark with its spin up (\(\uparrow\)) and down (\(\downarrow\)) inside a transversely polarized proton in the infinite momentum frame (Equation 1.33c).
2. Sivers effect [12]: Intrinsic transverse momentum \(\mathbf{k}_{T}\) of quarks inside hadron \(A\) requires that \(f_{a}(x_{a})\) in (1.18) be replaced by the probability density \({\cal P}_{a}(x_{a},\mathbf{k}_{T})\), which may depend on the spin of hadron \(A\) due to \(T\)-odd distribution functions (Equation 1.35).
3. Higher twist terms [13, 14, 15, 16, 17].

When the intrinsic transverse motion of quarks inside hadrons is taken into account, the quantum chromodynamics (QCD) factorization theorem isnot proven. Here we will assume that a factorization formula similar to (1.18) can be written

\[E_{h}\,\frac{{\rm d}\sigma}{{\rm d}^{3}\mathbf{P}_{h}} = \sum_{abc}\,\sum_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime}\beta\beta^{\prime}\gamma \gamma^{\prime}}\int{\rm d}x_{a}\int{\rm d}x_{b}\int{\rm d}^{2}\mathbf{k }_{T}\int{\rm d}^{2}\mathbf{k}^{\prime}_{T}\int{\rm d}^{2}\mathbf{\kappa}_{T}\,\frac{1}{\pi z} \tag{1.21}\] \[\times\,{\cal P}_{a}(x_{a},\mathbf{k}_{T})\,\rho^{a}_{ \alpha^{\prime}\alpha}\,{\cal P}_{b}(x_{b},\mathbf{k}^{\prime}_{T})\, \rho^{b}_{\beta^{\prime}\beta}\left(\frac{{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}}{{\rm d}\hat{t}} \right)_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime}\beta\beta^{\prime}\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}{\cal D }^{\gamma^{\prime}\gamma}_{h/c}(z,\mathbf{\kappa}_{T})\,,\]

where

\[\left(\frac{{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}}{{\rm d}\hat{t}}\right)_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime }\beta\beta^{\prime}\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{16\pi\hat{s}^{2}}\,\sum_{ \beta\delta}{\cal M}_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}{\cal M}^{*}_{\alpha^{\prime} \beta\gamma^{\prime}\delta}\,. \tag{1.22}\]

#### 1.3.1 Collins' Effect

Let us first consider the Collins' effect for single-spin asymmetries [11, 18]. Intrinsic transverse motion of the produced hadron \(h\) is considered, while the transverse momenta of quarks inside the initial-state hadrons are ignored. Thus Equation (1.21) becomes

\[E_{h}\,\frac{{\rm d}\sigma}{{\rm d}^{3}\mathbf{P}_{h}} = \sum_{abc}\,\sum_{\alpha\alpha^{\prime}\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}\int {\rm d}x_{a}\int{\rm d}x_{b}\int{\rm d}^{2}\mathbf{\kappa}_{T}\,\frac {1}{\pi z} \tag{1.23}\] \[\times\,f_{a}(x_{a})\,\rho^{a}_{\alpha^{\prime}\alpha}\,f_{b}(x_{ b})\left(\frac{{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}}{{\rm d}\hat{t}}\right)_{\alpha\alpha^{ \prime}\gamma\gamma^{\prime}}{\cal D}^{\gamma^{\prime}\gamma}_{h/c}(z,\mathbf{\kappa}_{T})\,,\]

and the elementary cross-sections are given by (1.19), with \(\mathbf{\kappa}_{T}\) retained. We are interested in transverse spin asymmetries \(\,{\rm d}\sigma(\mathbf{S}_{T})-\,{\rm d}\sigma(-\mathbf{S}_ {T})\). Therefore, since we are neglecting the intrinsic \(\mathbf{k}_{T}\) motion of quarks inside \(A\), the spin density matrix elements that contribute to the asymmetry are \(\rho^{a}_{+-}\) and \(\rho^{a}_{-+}\) (off-diagonal terms of \(s_{x}\) and \(s_{y}\) in Equation 1.31), and the contributing elementary cross-sections are \(\,{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}_{+-+-}=\,{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}_{-+-+}\) and \(\,{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}_{+--+}=\,{\rm d}\hat{\sigma}_{-++-}\).

