## Chapter 3 Analysis Method

### 3.1 General Analysis Steps

For both FY02 and FY03, analysis of data taken with (prototype)FPD involves the following steps:

1. Pedestal analysis and subtraction of pedestals. In general, detector electronics readouts produce non-zero values (pedestals) even in the absence of beams. It is important to understand the possible time dependence of pedestals (pedestal drift), so that the pedestals can be subtracted from the data in the subsequent analysis. This step could be done in hardware level, as for FY03 Pb-glass detectors (but not for SMD).
2. \(\pi^{0}\) event reconstruction. A software program is used to select di-photon events from FPD data and to reconstruct the invariant mass of the possible \(\pi^{0}\)s.
3. Calibration of detector. Adjust the software gain values of PMTs so the reconstructed \(\pi^{0}\) invariant mass has the correct value.
4. Understand possible background correction and efficiency of the reconstruction program.

