#### 3.5.1 Shower Shape

When a high-energy Electromagnetic particle (photon, \(e^{+}\), \(e^{-}\)) enters the Pb-glass detector, it will trigger an Electromagnetic shower ([29]). Two

Figure 3.2: neutral Pion reconstruction with SMD, run FY03

characteristic quantities of the material govern how the shower is developed in the material, the radiation length \(X_{0}\), the critical energy for electrons \(E_{c}\) and Moliere radius \(R_{M}\). For Pb-glass, \(X_{0}=2.5\) cm (from now on, when we speak about radiation length, we have in fact converted it to length, usually in unit of cm, by multiplying it with the density of the material), \(E_{c}=15.8\) MeV, and \(R_{M}=3.32\) cm.

The final Cerenkov lights are collected in the PMT tube and converted to the signal. As explained earlier, we need to extract both energy and position information of the incoming photons. The idea is then to fit the Pb-glass tower responses (a 2-D array of energy deposit in \(7\times 7\) Pb-glass towers) with the profile shape of the Electromagnetic shower.

However, keep in mind that what we measure in Pb-glass towers is not the Electromagnetic shower shape itself, but rather the integral of the shower within the Pb-glass volume. Furthermore, we assume that the longitudinal functional form of the shower is the same regardless of the photon energy, thus we integrate over the length of the Pb-glass detector from now on. The result is a 2-D function, with \(x\) and \(y\) as the transverse coordinates of photon fit location from the center of the Pb-glass.

In [36], the transverse Electromagnetic Shower shape was modeled by the following functions:\[G(x,y)=F(x+\frac{d}{2},y+\frac{d}{2})-F(x+\frac{d}{2},y-\frac{d}{2})-F(x-\frac{d}{2 },y+\frac{d}{2})+F(x-\frac{d}{2},y-\frac{d}{2}), \tag{3.5}\]

\[F(x,y)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_{i}\,\Bigg{(}\arctan\frac{xy}{b_{i}\sqrt{ b_{i}^{2}+x^{2}+y^{2}}}\Bigg{)}, \tag{3.6}\]

where \(d\) is the width of a Pb-glass detector, and the parameters \(a_{i}\) and \(b_{i}\) are:

Integrating \(G(x,y)\) over the entire \(x-y\) plane and demanding the result to be 1, we arrive at the condition that \(a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}=1\). The above values above were chosen with this restriction in mind. However, it is somewhat natural to have \(a_{3}=-0.1\) to be negative, as it resulted in negative value (albeit very small) far away from the center of shower.

